---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = 'https://media.huanyuchen.com/misc/fireworks/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Fireworks — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Every fireworks show we have watched from 2021 to 2026, from where we watched it: a harbor in Long Beach, a Coldplay finale at the Hollywood Bowl, July 4ths in Marina del Rey, Irvine and over the Potomac, Disneyland from the freeway, a hotel balcony in Waikīkī, and a drone show in Burbank.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2021–2026</p>
  <h1>Fireworks</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Eight shows, and where we were standing for each of them.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>8 fireworks shows · 1 drone show · 1 backyard · October 2021 – September 2026</strong><br />
    3 Fourths of July · 2 New Year's Eves · 1 concert finale · 1 theme park, from the freeway · 1 luau, from a balcony<br />
    Biggest: America's 250th over the Potomac · closest: our own fountains in the desert
  </div>

  <p>
    Fireworks are the one kind of show where your seat matters more than the show. The same shells look completely
    different over water, over trees, over a freeway or between two hotel towers. Going through five years of
    videos, I found more of them than I expected, each watched from somewhere different. Here they are in order,
    with the place we watched from, because that is what I actually remember.
  </p>

  <h2>A Coldplay finale, Hollywood Bowl · October 23, 2021</h2>
  <p>
    The first is the one I have already written about twice. At the end of Coldplay's set at the radio station's
    charity concert, during "A Sky Full of Stars", fireworks went up from behind the Bowl's shell, pink and gold
    over the trees on the hill, while the seats glowed with LED wristbands. The full night is in
    <a href="/misc/live-music">the live music post</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wcs-2021-fireworks.webp'} alt="Pink and gold fireworks bursting over the Hollywood Bowl at night, smoke drifting over the dark trees, the shell lit purple below and the audience glowing with small lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"A Sky Full of Stars", 10:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="nye-2021">New Year's Eve, Rainbow Harbor, Long Beach · December 31, 2021</h2>
  <p>
    Two months later I saw the year out, with two friends from USC, on the waterfront at Long Beach's Rainbow Harbor. At midnight the show
    started over the water, around the tall white lighthouse, lit purple, that stands on the harbor point. The
    video runs for thirteen minutes, with the smoke building up until the last gold bursts seemed to come out of a
    cloud, and every one of them reflected in the harbor among the moored boats.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'longbeach-nye.webp'} alt="A huge gold and pink firework bursting over a harbor at night, with a purple-lit lighthouse tower and moored boats below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The finale over the lighthouse, early on January 1, 2022.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="july4-2022">Fourth of July, Marina del Rey · 2022</h2>
  <p>
    The next Fourth was by the water at Marina del Rey, with three friends. One of them is now my wife, though at
    the time we were just friends. We were on the shore at dusk, waiting with everyone else, and a little after nine
    the show went up over the marina, with the lights of the far shore below it and the smoke
    turning pink.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'marina-del-rey.webp'} alt="A red and gold firework bursting above pink smoke and a light pole, over the marina at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Marina del Rey, July 4, 2022, a little after 9 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="july4-2024">Fourth of July, Irvine · 2024</h2>
  <p>
    Two years later we watched Irvine's show at Mike Ward Community Park in Woodbridge, in a big crowd on the
    lawns. It is a park, not a harbor, so the fireworks came up over a line of trees, and the
    finale filled the whole sky with pink.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'irvine-finale.webp'} alt="A huge pink and white firework finale filling the night sky above dark tree silhouettes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The finale, 9:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="drone-show-2024">A drone show, Burbank · November 9, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    Not fireworks, but it belongs here. One November evening in Burbank, a swarm of lit drones rose over the trees
    and drew pictures in the sky: a bouquet of flowers, a swan, birds in flight, a ballerina. We watched from a park
    lawn lit with rows of little electric candles.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'drone-lawn.webp'} alt="A bouquet of flowers drawn in the dusk sky by lit drones, above trees and a park lawn glowing with rows of small candles, with a crowd watching." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Flowers over the lawn, 6:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    It is quieter than fireworks, and stranger, and nobody needs to sweep up afterwards.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'drone-swan.webp'} alt="A swan drawn in the night sky by hundreds of lit drones, white with an orange beak, above trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A swan, 7:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'drone-ballerina.webp'} alt="A ballerina in a blue tutu drawn in the night sky by lit drones, above trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A ballerina, a minute later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="disneyland-i5-2024">Disneyland, from the freeway · November 17, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    The least planned show on this list. Driving on the I-5 one Sunday night, we passed Anaheim just as
    Disneyland's fireworks went up, and for a minute or two there were gold bursts above the overpasses, in the
    middle of traffic.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i5-disneyland.webp'} alt="Gold fireworks above a freeway at night, with an overpass, streetlights and tail lights ahead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Disneyland's fireworks over the I-5, 9:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="nye-2024">New Year's Eve, San Gabriel · 2024</h2>
  <p>
    At midnight going into 2025 we were at a friend's home in San Gabriel, where the neighbourhood set off its own fireworks, and for a few
    minutes the sky over the rooftops and power lines filled with bursts and smoke.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'san-gabriel-nye.webp'} alt="A gold firework bursting in a smoky night sky, crossed by power lines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Just after midnight, January 1, 2025.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Our own, in the desert · July 2025</h2>
  <p>
    In 2025 we did it ourselves. On July 3 we stopped at a fireworks stand in Orange County, under a city poster
    explaining which fireworks are legal and which are a crime, and came away with a back seat full of fountains,
    wands and sparklers.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'haul.webp'} alt="A car seat piled with boxed fireworks: fountains, wands and sparklers in bright packaging." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The haul, July 3.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zero-tolerance.webp'} alt="A bilingual poster at the fireworks stand: zero tolerance, illegal fireworks are a crime, legal ones only July 2, 3 and 4." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rules, posted at the stand.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two nights later, at a rental in the desert near Twentynine Palms, we lit them in the sand.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'backyard-fountain.webp'} alt="Two lit fountain wands spraying white sparks and smoke over sand at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>In the sand, July 5, 11:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>America at 250, over the Potomac · July 4, 2026</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest show by far. For the 250th Fourth of July we stood on the Key Bridge in Washington, packed in along
    the railing, and watched the fireworks rise from behind Roosevelt Island, with the Kennedy Center lit below and
    the Washington Monument to one side. Every burst arrived twice, once in the sky and once on the river. The whole
    night is in <a href="/misc/july4-2026">Fireworks for the 250th</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-03.webp'} alt="Red fireworks bursting over the river, with long red reflections on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A red wave, and its reflection.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-09.webp'} alt="A huge blue firework above the island with its reflection on the river and the Georgetown waterfront lit at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last blue burst.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>A luau next door, Waikīkī · September 11, 2026</h2>
  <p>
    And the easiest seat of all. Every Friday evening, the Hilton Hawaiian Village ends its luau with fireworks over
    the beach. That Friday we were staying at Ka Laʻi, a few hundred meters away, and watched the whole show from our
    balcony, with a boat lit up on the water below it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-fireworks.webp'} alt="Fireworks bursting red, green and gold over the beach at night, with pink fireworks rising from the shore, a lit boat on the water and hotel towers at the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the balcony, 7:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Where to stand</h2>
  <p>
    Lined up, the lesson is obvious: the best seat is over water. A harbor, a river or a bay doubles every burst, and
    gives the smoke somewhere to go. The second-best seat is high up, a bridge or a balcony. And the most memorable,
    somehow, are the unplanned ones: a freeway, a neighbourhood at midnight, a pile of fountains in the sand.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Most images are frames from phone videos. Times are local. Photo locations have been
    removed from the published images.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
  <Comments />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
